# Caddyfile — reverse proxy + automatic HTTPS for ai.dhaaselab.com, fronting the real
# Jarvis server (server.py) at http://localhost:8765.
#
# Uses a DNS-01 ACME challenge via Cloudflare (see caddy/README.md) rather than the usual
# HTTP-01 challenge — deliberate, because ai.dhaaselab.com's DNS record points at this
# machine's Tailscale IP (100.x.x.x), which isn't publicly reachable. DNS-01 only needs to
# prove control of the domain via a temporary DNS TXT record, so it works even though the
# machine itself is only reachable over the tailnet — the whole point of this setup (see
# REMOTE_ACCESS.md): a real trusted certificate without exposing anything to the public
# internet.
#
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN is read from the environment by start_caddy.ps1 (pulled from the
# main project's .env, same as every other secret here) — never hardcoded in this file.

ai.dhaaselab.com {
	tls {
		dns cloudflare {env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
	}
	reverse_proxy localhost:8765
}

# Local HTTPS on the LAN (Remote Access rework — see REMOTE_ACCESS.md's "Local HTTPS on the
# LAN" section) — a real trusted cert for the machine's LAN IP, issued by mkcert's local CA
# rather than a public ACME challenge (a LAN IP can't get a public cert). This is what lets
# the phone get a secure context (mic input, push, PWA install) while on the home network,
# without needing Tailscale/Twingate connected at all — those stay reserved for being away
# from home.
#
# Two addresses listed on purpose: 192.168.1.147 is the home LAN address; 138.247.39.30 was
# added temporarily while this machine was on a different network away from home (see
# task.md/REMOTE_ACCESS.md's note on this) — remove it once that's no longer needed, or add
# a new address the same way if the home LAN IP ever changes. Reserve whichever IPs matter
# as static DHCP leases in the router so this doesn't drift.
#
# IMPORTANT: 138.247.39.30 is NOT a private/LAN-range address — do not open a firewall rule
# for port 8443 on any network profile broader than "Private" while this line is active,
# or Jarvis becomes reachable from more than just this trusted network.
https://192.168.1.147:8443, https://138.247.39.30:8443 {
	tls lan_cert.pem lan_key.pem
	reverse_proxy localhost:8765
}
